# Rank 1086 / CVE-2023-3975: draw.io's Electron desktop process spawned
# external binaries with argv arrays built from file paths the renderer
# supplied, so a value beginning with a dash reaches the target program as an
# option rather than a filename (CWE-78 as classified by the advisory, though
# the shape is argument injection, CWE-88). main/webapp/electron.js has
#
#   var child = spawn('attrib', ['+h', draftFileName]);
#
# among several spawn calls whose argv array holds a value the main process did
# not originate.
#
# This CVE has no in-repo before/after code pair: the ChangeLog entry for the
# fix release states the remediation plainly -- "Moves electron.js out of
# drawio project into desktop" -- and the fix commit 8ec95cb03e deletes
# electron.js (2454 lines) and electron-preload.js from this repository
# entirely rather than patching them, moving the Electron main process to
# jgraph/drawio-desktop. The one in-repo code delta the same release carries,
# tightening export.js's navigator.userAgent check for
# " electron/" to also require " draw.io/", is real but is UI/CSP hardening,
# not demonstrably the sink this advisory names, and pinning it as the fix
# would overstate what the diff supports.
#
# ATTENTION, and the vocabulary gap is concrete and measured, a third instance
# of the orphaned-sink cause recorded for ranks 1050 and 1085.
#
#  - spawn's argv-array elements are bound as code.CommandExecution, a CWE-78
#    sink, only when the invoked command is a shell (cmd.exe, powershell,
#    /bin/sh, /bin/bash, or an explicit shell: true), correctly so: spawn
#    without a shell target does not reinterpret its argv as shell syntax, and
#    binding every spawn(cmd, [args]) call this way would flag the documented,
#    safe way to avoid shell injection in Node.js. Measured directly:
#    spawn('cp', [tainted, dest]) reports nothing, and the identical call with
#    cmd.exe as the target does report, VYQL-INJ-002 CWE-78.
#  - What that correct scoping leaves uncovered is CWE-88: a value in that same
#    argv array reaching a non-shell binary that itself parses leading-dash
#    arguments as options, which is exactly attrib's +h argument and this CVE's
#    shape. code.CommandOptionInjectionSink exists for this and is unbound for
#    JavaScript's spawn family entirely, on top of rank 1050's finding that no
#    rule in vyql/packs consumes that sink in any language regardless.
#
# Scanning electron.js in isolation, without the renderer-side call sites that
# would establish draftFileName and filePath as tainted, reports nothing; the
# file was not scanned as a whole corpus because its removal from HEAD means
# the vulnerable revision is only reachable by checking out the fix's parent.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap

test "rank1086: spawn's argv array carries a value the process did not originate"
  lang javascript
  file src/main/webapp/electron.js
  reject VYQL-INJ-002
  code
  ```
  const { spawn } = require('child_process');

  function hideDraftFile(draftFileName) {
    var child = spawn('attrib', ['+h', draftFileName]);
    child.on('error', function (err) {
      console.log('hiding draft file error: ' + err);
    });
  }
  ```

# The scoping pair. Both calls put the same tainted value in the argv array;
# only the target program differs, and that is the whole difference between a
# genuine CWE-78 shell-reinterpretation sink and a non-shell binary that spawn
# alone does not make injectable.

test "rank1086: the same argv element passed to a non-shell binary"
  lang javascript
  file non_shell.js
  reject VYQL-INJ-002
  code
  ```
  const { spawn } = require('child_process');

  function handler(req, res) {
    const path = req.query.path;
    spawn('cp', [path, '/var/backups/']);
  }
  ```

test "rank1086: the same argv element passed to a shell target"
  lang javascript
  file shell_target.js
  expect VYQL-INJ-002
  code
  ```
  const { spawn } = require('child_process');

  function handler(req, res) {
    const path = req.query.path;
    spawn('cmd.exe', ['/c', path]);
  }
  ```
